## Fastify Prototype Poisoning Prevention

### The Core Mechanism

Fastify uses the **`secure-json-parse`** package instead of the native `JSON.parse`. This is wired up in **`lib/content-type-parser.js`**:

```js
// lib/content-type-parser.js, line 5
const { parse: secureJsonParse } = require('secure-json-parse')
```

The `ContentTypeParser` constructor (line 32) accepts two poisoning-control callbacks and passes them to `getDefaultJsonParser`:

```js
function ContentTypeParser (bodyLimit, onProtoPoisoning, onConstructorPoisoning) {
  this[kDefaultJsonParse] = getDefaultJsonParser(onProtoPoisoning, onConstructorPoisoning)
  // also: uses Map instead of plain object to avoid prototype hijack on parser storage itself
  this.customParsers = new Map()
  ...
}
```

### The Default JSON Parser

**`getDefaultJsonParser`** (lines 306–368) builds `parseOptions` from those two policy strings and calls `secureJsonParse` on every request body:

```js
function getDefaultJsonParser (onProtoPoisoning, onConstructorPoisoning) {
  const parseOptions = {
    protoAction: onProtoPoisoning,
    constructorAction: onConstructorPoisoning
  }

  return function defaultJsonParser (req, body, done) {
    if (body.length === 0) {
      done(new FST_ERR_CTP_EMPTY_JSON_BODY(), undefined)
      return
    }
    try {
      done(null, secureJsonParse(body, parseOptions))
    } catch {
      done(new FST_ERR_CTP_INVALID_JSON_BODY(), undefined)
    }
  }
}
```

`secure-json-parse` detects and reacts to `__proto__` and `constructor.prototype` keys before any object mutation occurs.

---

### Configuration

Both options are exposed as top-level `Fastify()` server options and are validated by the config schema (`lib/config-validator.js`):

| Option | Type | Values | Default (v3) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `onProtoPoisoning` | `string` | `"error"` · `"remove"` · `"ignore"` | `"error"` |
| `onConstructorPoisoning` | `string` | `"error"` · `"remove"` · `"ignore"` | `"error"` |

```js
// Block (throw 400)
const fastify = Fastify({
  onProtoPoisoning: 'error',          // default — rejects the request
  onConstructorPoisoning: 'error'     // default — rejects constructor attacks too
})

// Strip silently (key is deleted, parse continues)
const fastify = Fastify({
  onProtoPoisoning: 'remove',
  onConstructorPoisoning: 'remove'
})

// Allow through (dangerous — opt-in only)
const fastify = Fastify({
  onProtoPoisoning: 'ignore',
  onConstructorPoisoning: 'ignore'
})
```

The `"ignore"` setting triggers a **`FSTSEC001`** security warning (imported in `content-type-parser.js` line 30) to alert developers they're disabling a security control.

